<article class="env-lemma" id="087G">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/087G">Lemma <span data-tag="087G">76.39.5</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $f : X \to B$ be a morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$. Let $U \subset B$ be an open subspace. Assume </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$B$ is quasi-compact and quasi-separated, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$U$ is quasi-compact, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$f : X \to B$ is proper, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$f^{-1}(U) \to U$ us an isomorphism. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Then there exists a $U$-admissible blowup $B' \to B$ which dominates $X$, i.e., such that there exists a factorization $B' \to X \to B$ of the blowup morphism. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Lemma <a href="/tag/0GUW" data-tag="0GUW">76.39.3</a> we may find a $U$-admissible blowup $B' \to B$ such that the strict transform $X'$ maps isomorphically to $B'$. Then we can use $B' = X' \to X$ as the factorization. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>